A||||14||GT Bastard||Why I bought a Fiero..... ||||2||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||04-22-2000||01:45 PM||||Just thought I'd take a few minutes to express why I sold my '94 Z28 Camaro and devoted all my efforts to a wrecked '87 GT.  Don't get me wrong, there's something cool about being able to roast a new set of 245's down the rim, but there's something unique about the Fiero.  The part that really bugged me was the fact that there seemed to be a Camaro on just about every street corner.  Any damn fool can go buy a brand new F-body or Mustang and have a fast car, but it's a whole lot more satisfying to turn a relatively slow car into a fast one.  The funny thing that I get more compliments on the Fiero than I did the Z28.  Anyhow.... My fiero went from a repairables lot to my daily driver, and I'm more proud of this car than I was of my Camaro.   ok, I think I'm done now..... later people.||||reg||2
Z||000001||gixxer||04-22-2000||03:42 PM||||Fieros are VERY rare up here in the Puget Sound area Washington state,at least where I live. When I got rid of my CRX last Sunday, I gave a sigh of relief. Now I don't have to deal with the ricers. <BR>With only 30K miles, but with much mechanical neglect, I'm happy to say that my new Fiero is shaping up very nicely. Gotta find some new front tires now as it still has the original fronts on, and are they HARD!<BR>Cheers.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TwisT||04-22-2000||06:58 PM||||GTBastard,  you've just pinpointed EXACTLY why most Fiero owners love their cars.  I come from a family of motor-heads and I've personally owned everything from a chopped/tubbed/454-powered '47 Chevy Coupe to an '82 Mustang with a Hi-Po 289 swap (former 4-cylinder).  Is my '88 Fiero GT the fastest car I've ever owned?  Not by a LONG shot.  But it's uniqueness and exotic looks puts every 'Stang and Camaro to rest.  <P>Ever count how many Mustangs and Camaros you see on your way to work???  LOL  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||phillip||04-22-2000||07:21 PM||||i've said before that i got mine to sort of match and replace as a daily driver a '74 vette. but like with any thing you have you fool with it and get more info on it. i like the possibilities presented by this car. it has a unique look (the coupes anyway), it's easy to sort of customise bodywise, and if i ever get the resourses together for an engine change, performance to match it's looks.<P>i like it enough that i recently began the process of getting another ('84 indy) and if i could find one and afford it i'd get  a convertible too (the insurance man would love me).||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||04-23-2000||02:53 AM||||I got mine because I sold my last car and I needed a car QUICKLY!!!!!<P>Little did I know when the last owner told me "you are going to LOVE this car...."  that I WOULD, and HOW!<P>I love them because like you guys, they are O-RI-GI-NAL!<P>They don't look like every friggin car out there with a rear end to the asphalt, a spoiler the length of the car and super fast blinkers.<P>And despite what people may think, they run GREAT, just as long as you keep up on matienence, just like any other car on the market.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sdm1964||04-23-2000||08:20 AM||||I fell in love with them when i worked at a dealership. But a young guy and a growing family, you know the story.  I worked 2 years on my Z28 to have some kid pull out in front of me. It can be fixed but i needed another car fast, that is when i decided to get my 85 GT. True not the fastest car I've owned but I have the only fiero in town!!!! I think it is more fun to drive than the Z28 and that is why I bought another fiero, 86 SE and i am selling the camaro. Fun to drive and not one on every street corner...dare to be different.. thats me<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000006||TooLowFiero||04-23-2000||11:19 AM||||im in the same boat as some of you.. i needed a car FAST...my (now brother inlaw)had an 86 2m4 with no power options and 118,000 miles on it..it sat next to his house for two years.. i ask him if he wanted it still he said no 100 bucks and its yours..so i bought it.. daily driver for a year with only about 100 bucks invested into it..so i bought another 1.. now a parts car..found it had cracked head so im parting it out.its an 84 BTW..then i found another one.. 85 se v-6 4 speed runs great only 85000 miles and looks great needed a clutch did that this weekend.. soo all in all ive paid 550 bucks for all three of my fieros..ok im done rambling..Happy fiero'ing<P><BR>TooLow||||reg||10
Z||000007||Homicidal||04-24-2000||01:56 AM||||TooLowFiero:<P>PLEASE check this post out and see if you have this piece!<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/001914.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/001914.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Haze_Performance||04-24-2000||02:43 AM||||I liked Fiero's since I was a kid... Ive owned many different cars. About 5/6 months ago my buddy (who works at a VW dealer) called me and said we just took in a trade-in from a guy. The guy's father just passed away and he traded in his 98 Escort and 85 Fiero 2M4. The Fiero had 36,000 miles on it and was as near mint as I have ever seen a Fiero at a dealer. It had been in storage for the last 9 years. Well he sold it to me for what he gave the guy ($2500). This is the best $2500 I have spent in my life. Fiero's are a blast to drive. I sold two of my Mustangs to put more time and $ into it. Ok...Ok...I still have a 92 Mustang, Im not ready to get rid of it yet, I need a fast car.....(Im sure Ill sell it as soon as the V8 gos into the Fiero................sorry for the long post..   ||||reg||10
Z||000009||kevin||04-25-2000||02:09 AM||||I felt compelled to share my thoughts on why I love my Fiero. I am honost in saying I had other sports/GT cars;namely a 79 Fierbird, 66 GT hi-po Mustang, 84 Corvette, and a 84 Ferrari. Here's the truth, my 85 SE Duke provides me with as much, if not more, pleasure than the above vehicles I owned. Why? Cost per miles, absolute perfict fit for my 6'2" frame, as well as having all the comfort amenities I could want(from the factory) all complimented with stellar and perdictable handling. One sure fire no miss test you can take yourself to determine if the Fiero is the car for you, is to ask yourself this question as you leave for work in the morning, 'Do yu feel happy walking to your car?' If you answer in the affirmative, if the Fiero makes you smile as you pull away and shift your first gear, if at that time you feel sorry for all the poor slugs and slobs who trundle around in 4 door BLOB cars and ill-handling and clumsy lummox riding SUV's, then you understand the feeling of owning a car that few other cars ever manufactured can ever hope to boast about. You own a car that can be modified to fit your individual tastes, or not modified at all, that provides a feeling of controll and safety that can be shared with like minded friends across the USA and beyond. Few cars have the same ambience and charm.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Haze_Performance||04-25-2000||02:32 AM||||Is there a way I could trade my Fiero for a 84 Ferrari??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> I think I myself would be MUCH happier walking out of the house to goto work and jumping in my Ferrari...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000011||kevin||04-25-2000||09:59 AM||||The Ferrari was way cool to drive,and a helluva chick magnant. However, there always seemed to be areas of quality controll that was never really satisfied. Speedometer cable always "cliked", power windows were dreadfully slow in operation, tune-ups at the dealership were "you got to be kidding..." expensive. If I wowed the chickes on Saturday that I drove a Ferrari, there was no way to guarantee I could give her a ride the following Monday. The Fiero on the other hand had all the same mechanical foundations, with none of the same niggling problems. Not the least of which  would be a chicks hanging around thinking that I would pay for all her dresses, perfume, trips to L.A. etc. The Fiero allows me the opportunity to reflect my charm and wit onto the fairer sex without any pretentions. No regrets on the Ferrari. But then again, I do dream about owning another. It would pretty sitting next to my Fiero.   ||||reg||1
Z||000012||mshill||04-25-2000||12:40 PM||||I initially bought my 86SE last October as a donor for a kit car rebody.  I have wanted to build a kit ever since I was 17 (well over 20 years ago), and my dad had a 66' VW bug that I wanted to turn into a Sterling or 917 copy.<P>Well,  the White 86SE was in such nice shape (interior/exterior) that I decided to keep it more or less stock and rebuild the engine and keep it as my alternate driver while building my kit on another donor, 86GT which I picked up in December for $500.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000013||Racer 1||04-25-2000||02:13 PM||||Very nicely said KEVIN. I always smile when I go out to my fiero. I always park it far out in the wal-mart parking lot so I can take a long time to look at my fiero from every angle. Everytime I get into my fiero I feel like a YF-22 pilot getting into his jet "excited". When I first got my fiero it was in bad shape. But the car grows on you thru thick and thin.<P> <B>Homicidal</B>, GM updated that part its a solid cast piece now. Mine broke also just ge the updated part it 100% better.<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000014||lennyb4||04-25-2000||02:27 PM||||I always have liked the fiero but it wasnt until last year i got my chance to own one. Believe it or not i got my fiero through a bet with a friends co-worker (long story). It does need alot of work to it but like you guys said it grows on you. Just cant wait until its all done...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
